TI  - Decreased adaptability and increased social media use of Chinese female students during COVID-19: a multivariate latent growth curve model
T2  - CURRENT PSYCHOLOGY
AB  - College students' adaptability has been influenced by COVID-19-related management strategies. Besides, students' social media use and fear of missing out (FOMO) increased during the COVID-19 lockdown. However, the dynamic changes in adaptability and their longitudinal associations with social media use and the FOMO in female university students are unclear. Therefore, this study employed the Chinese versions of the Freshmen Adaptability Scale, Fear of Missing Out Scale, and Social Media Use Questionnaire to investigate (i) how adaptability, social media use, and FOMO change over time and (ii) whether there is a significant longitudinal link among female college students' adaptability, social media use, and FOMO. We employed convenience sampling to enlist a cohort of 335 female college student volunteers who actively engaged in a sequence of three-wave surveys for the present study (Mean Age = 19.49, Standard Deviation = 1.92). The results revealed that female college students' FOMO (beta slope = -0.258, p = .013) and adaptability (beta slope = -0.381, p = .014) decreased significantly over time, while their social media use increased significantly over time (beta slope = 0.200, p = .027) based on the average initial score of 5.295. Additionally, the results showed that FOMO at baseline played a directly mediating role in the relationship between adaptability and social media use at baseline. Furthermore, the results also showed that a greater rate of decrease in adaptability was associated with a lower rate of decrease in FOMO. The study has revealed dynamic patterns in adaptability, social media usage, and FOMO among female college students throughout the COVID-19 lockdown, also uncovering the psychological processes interlinking these factors. These insights not only yield critical strategies for effectively countering the negative effects of future epidemics but also highlight the necessity for educational leaders to provide enhanced support and resources specifically for female college students. Such targeted support is vital in ensuring their well-being and academic success during times of crisis.
