TI  - Social anxiety, maladaptive cognition, mobile phone addiction, and perceived social support: A moderated mediation model
T2  - JOURNAL OF PSYCHOLOGY IN AFRICA
AB  - This study tested the mediation effect of maladaptive cognition in the association between social anxiety and mobile phone addiction, and the moderating role of perceived social support in that relationship. A sample of 468 Chinese university students (female = 43.6%; age range = 17 to 23 years, SD = 1.6 years) were study participants. They completed measures of social anxiety, mobile phone addiction, maladaptive cognition, and perceived social support. Results revealed that maladaptive cognition mediated the link between social anxiety and mobile phone addiction. Moreover, perceived social support moderated the relationship between maladaptive cognition and mobile phone addiction. However, the moderation impact was stronger for individuals with high perceived social support than for those with low perceived social support. These findings emphasise the necessity of identifying the mechanisms that moderate the mediated paths between social anxiety and mobile phone addiction among college students.
