TI  - The impact of depression on parental control and problematic mobile phone use: A person-centered approach
T2  - CURRENT PSYCHOLOGY
AB  - This study delves into the mechanisms linking parental control to problematic mobile phone use among adolescents, differentiating between those who exhibit problematic usage and those who do not. The study involved 1,113 Chinese high school students, among whom 559 were identified as problematic mobile phone users (16.75 +/- 0.90 years old, 20.04% female), and 554 were unproblematic mobile phone users (16.96 +/- 6.45 years old, 23.47% female). The latent profiles analysis was calculated to test the parental control profiles and the mediation analysis was calculated to test the mediation role of depression in the relationship between different combinations of psychological control (PPC) and parental behavioral control (PBC) on problematic mobile phone use. The findings suggest that, in both user groups, depression is a critical mediating factor connecting PPC and PBC to problematic mobile phone use. Specifically, in unproblematic users, depression mediates the relationship between profiles 2 (low PPC, high PBC) and 3 (high PPC, low PBC) and problematic mobile phone use. Conversely, in problematic mobile phone users, depression serves as a mediator between profiles 3 (high PPC, low PBC) and 4 (high PPC, high PBC) and mobile phone use. These findings offer insights into the dynamics within a Confucian cultural context and may hold relevance for other societies characterized by strong parental authority and digital dependence.
