AU  - Meilani, ER
AU  - Nuryana, Z
AU  - Wijaya, TT
TI  - Digital literacy, self-efficacy, and digital well-being: direct and indirect effects among final-year university students
T2  - CURRENT PSYCHOLOGY
AB  - The integration of digital technologies into daily life has reshaped communication, learning, and social interaction, presenting both opportunities and challenges for sustaining digital well-being. This study examined the direct effects of digital literacy; covering technical, cognitive, and socio-emotional competencies and self-efficacy; comprising initiative, effort, and persistence on three dimensions of digital well-being: digital satisfaction, digital wellness, and safe and responsible behaviour. It also investigated the indirect effects of digital literacy through self-efficacy. Data were collected from 341 final-year undergraduate students at a private Indonesian university and analysed using Partial Least Squares Structural Equation Modelling (PLS-SEM). Findings revealed that digital literacy significantly predicted all three dimensions, indicating its robust statistical association with informed, ethical, and balanced technology use. Self-efficacy was positively related to digital wellness but negatively to safe and responsible behaviour, and showed no link with digital satisfaction. Mediation tests indicated no indirect effects through self-efficacy. The results underscore the importance of comprehensive digital literacy programs and realistic confidence-building initiatives in higher education to support healthier and safer digital engagement.
