TI  - Links between image-based sexual abuse and mental health in childhood among young adult social media users
T2  - CHILD ABUSE & NEGLECT
AB  - Background: Image-based sexual abuse (IBSA) involves harm inflicted upon children through creation, distribution, and misuse of explicit images. IBSA includes scenarios where explicit ages are produced, shared, and misused by both adults and other youth. Objective: To examine the impact of IBSA, independent of child sexual abuse and adversity, drug overdoses, suicide attempts and self-injuries before the age of 18 with a focus on sexual gender minority youth. Participants and setting: Young adult social media users, aged 18 to 28 (n = 2630) from across United States. Methods: Participants were recruited through social media and completed an online survey. is cross-sectional-asking young adults to reflect back on experiences prior to age 18. Data collected between February 27, 2024 and April 1, 2024. Results: IBSA was associated with elevated odds of mental health impact, independent of in sexual abuse and adversity. Gender minority participants were more likely than cisgender ticipants to report IBSA; sexual minority youth more likely than those identifying exclusively heterosexual to report IBSA. Gender minority youth with IBSA had increased odds overdoses (aOR 3.0, p = .053) and suicide attempts (aOR 3.1, p < .001), controlling for factors. Among sexual minority youth, IBSA was associated with increased odds of drug overdoses (aOR 3.9, p = .002), suicide attempts (aOR 2.4, p < .001) and non-suicidal self-injury (aOR = .001). Conclusions: Youth serving professionals should be aware that IBSA is associated with increased public health problems. Vulnerability to IBSA should be included among the concerns nicians and educators screen for, particularly among vulnerable populations.
