AU  - Ma, X
AU  - Yin, L
AU  - Peng, LY
TI  - More media multitasking, more boredom: the moderating role of trait mindfulness
T2  - CURRENT PSYCHOLOGY
AB  - Although previous studies have demonstrated reciprocal influences between boredom and media multitasking in the short term, the long-term relationship between these constructs remains unclear. Additionally, moderating factors influencing the strength of this relationship have yet to be fully explored. To address these gaps, the current study employed a two-wave longitudinal design with a six-month interval to examine the long-term relationship between boredom and media multitasking, as well as the moderating role of trait mindfulness. A total of 1,243 college students (M-age = 18.54 years, SD = 0.74, 71.3% girls) from six universities in China participated in this study. Results indicated that, after controlling for age, gender, grade, and autoregressive effects: (a) prior boredom did not significantly predict subsequent media multitasking, but prior media multitasking significantly positively predicted subsequent boredom; and (b) trait mindfulness did not moderate the relationship between prior boredom and subsequent media multitasking. However, it did moderate the relationship between prior media multitasking and subsequent boredom. Specifically, the positive predictive effect of prior media multitasking on subsequent boredom was observed only among individuals with higher levels of trait mindfulness. These findings extend the existing literature and provide practical implications for preventing and addressing boredom among college students in the digital age.
