TI  - Dynamics of attachment insecurity to young adult problematic social media use: an ecological momentary assessment study
T2  - INFORMATION COMMUNICATION & SOCIETY
AB  - The growing issue of young adults grappling with problematic social media use (PSMU) has emerged as a significant health concern. While a link to insecure attachment has been noted, the precise mechanism linking the two is not well understood. This study collected data from 106 young adults (55.8% female, M = 20.88, SD = 1.69) with PSMU at four daily intervals over 11 days to explore the dynamics between trait insecure attachment and state attachment insecurity following negative events, and their impact on social media craving and engagement. Multilevel moderated mediation models indicated that state attachment insecurity mediates the effect of negative interpersonal events on craving, moderated by trait insecure attachment. This underscores the profound influence of attachment insecurity dynamics on the development of PSMU. Moreover, the study identified unique social media engagement patterns linked to attachment anxiety and avoidance, shedding light on how these psychological constructs distinctly influence online behaviors. These findings contribute to advancing our understanding of PSMU's development and are instrumental in crafting targeted interventions to mitigate the adverse outcomes associated with excessive social media use.
