AU  - Reed, P
AU  - Arin, N
TI  - How personal, social, and cognitive factors combine to shape Thai college students' well-being in the social media landscape
T2  - COGENT PSYCHOLOGY
AB  - This study examined how personal, social, and cognitive factors related to well-being among college students in Chiang Mai, Thailand withinin the context of social media use. A total of 612 participants aged 18-24 years (M-age = 19.54, SDage = 1.24; 67.5% female) were recruited using convenience sampling. A Kruskal-Wallis test assessed group differences in well-being based on time spent on social media. Structural equation modeling (SEM) examined relationships between perceived peer pressure, social comparison orientation, fear of missing out, growth mindset, and well-being. Multiple regression and simple slope method testedgrowth mindset as a moderator. Perceived peer pressure (beta = -0.10, p < 0.05) and social comparison orientation (beta = -0.11, p < 0.01) was significantly negatively associated with well-being, while fear of missing out was positively associated with well-being (beta = 0.20, p < 0.01). Growth mindset was strongly positively associated with well-being (beta = 0.85, p < 0.01), but did not moderate the associations with social media variables. Growth mindset is a strong correlate of well-being, highlighting potential for use in interventions. Social media-related factors were also significantly associated with well-being, underscoring the need for targeted interventions in the digital context.
